        <p>
          If you are shopping for a peristaltic pump, you will meet the same two pump head names again and again: YZ15 and YZ35. They look similar, mount on the same drives, and work on the same squeezing-tube principle — yet they are designed for different halves of the flow spectrum. Choose YZ15 for the wrong reason and you will struggle to transfer liters per minute; choose YZ35 for the wrong reason and you will be fighting for fine control at milliliters per minute. Getting the head right is the difference between a pump that feels built for your process and one you fight every day.
        </p>
        <p>
          This guide compares the YZ15 and YZ35 pump heads across tube compatibility, flow and speed range, accuracy, application fit and price positioning — then gives you a five-point decision checklist and answers the questions buyers ask most. By the end, you should be able to name the right head for your flow, tube and budget without a sales call.
        </p>

        <h2 id="what-are">What Are the YZ15 and YZ35 Pump Heads?</h2>
        <p>
          Both heads belong to the YZ series, the standard workhorse family of peristaltic pump heads used across laboratory, dosing and light industrial equipment. A YZ head is a mechanical module containing rollers, a rotor and a curved tube track; it mounts on a separate drive (stepper, brushless DC or AC motor with a controller) and the drive's speed determines the flow. YZ heads are typically available in 1, 2 and 4-channel versions, so one drive can pump several fluids in parallel for filling lines and multi-channel dispensing.
        </p>
        <p>
          The practical difference between YZ15 and YZ35 comes down to the envelope of tubing each one can accept — specifically the tube's internal diameter and wall size. The head's track radius, roller spacing and occlusion are machined around a band of tube sizes, and that single difference cascades into flow capacity, precision and the applications each head suits. In most catalogs, YZ15 is the small-bore head and YZ35 is the large-bore head, with some overlap in the middle.
        </p>

        <h2 id="tubing">Tube Compatibility: 13#–25# vs Larger Bore Tubing</h2>
        <p>
          Tubing in the peristaltic world is identified by a size number (13#, 14#, 19#, 16#, 25#, 17#, 18#, 35#, 36# and so on). The number maps to a combination of internal and external diameter, and each pump head is designed for a specific band of these sizes. This is the first and most important filter in your decision.
        </p>
        <p>
          The <strong>YZ15</strong> head accepts the standard small-to-medium sizes — 13#, 14#, 19#, 16#, 25#, 17# and 18# — which covers internal diameters from roughly 0.8 mm (13#) up to about 7.9 mm (18#). That is a wide and genuinely useful band: it includes the classic laboratory tubing sizes and the mid-range 25# used in many dosing applications.
        </p>
        <p>
          The <strong>YZ35</strong> head is built for larger bore tubing — 25# and up, typically into the 35# and 36# class with internal diameters around 9.7 mm and beyond. The larger tube holds more fluid per revolution of the rotor, which is exactly what you want for high-volume transfer. Some YZ35 heads can also take mid-range sizes, but they are happiest with big-bore tubing, and fitting a tiny 13# tube in a YZ35 track usually produces poor occlusion and unstable flow. As a rule of thumb: if your tube size is 25# or smaller, YZ15 covers it; if you plan to run 35#-class tubing, you need a YZ35.
        </p>

        <h2 id="flow">Flow Rate and Speed Range</h2>
        <p>
          A pump head has no fixed flow rate — flow is the product of tube internal diameter, rotor speed and the head's occlusion setting. That is why manufacturers publish flow matrices instead of a single number, and why the same drive can deliver wildly different flows depending on which head and tube you mount. Still, the two heads occupy clearly different flow bands.
        </p>
        <p>
          On a typical variable-speed drive (0.1–600 rpm is a common range), a <strong>YZ15</strong> head with small tubing delivers from well under a milliliter per minute up to roughly 4,500 ml/min with the largest tube at top speed. The small-bore end of the YZ15 range is where precision dosing lives: with 13# or 14# tubing, you can meter a few milliliters per minute at a comfortable mid-speed, which keeps accuracy high and tube wear low.
        </p>
        <p>
          A <strong>YZ35</strong> head, by contrast, is built to move volume. With 35#-class tubing at high speed, YZ35-class heads are commonly rated to around 12,000 ml/min (12 L/min) — roughly three times the ceiling of a YZ15. The trade-off is that the same drive speed moves far more fluid per revolution, so fine low-flow control is harder to achieve. If most of your operating life sits above 1–2 L/min, the YZ35 is the natural fit; if you live in the milliliters range, the YZ15 gives you a much larger usable speed window.
        </p>

        <h2 id="accuracy">Accuracy: Where Each Head Earns Its Precision</h2>
        <p>
          Both heads can deliver repeatable flow — with a stepper drive and fresh, correctly sized tubing, repeatability of around ±1% is typical for the YZ family. The difference is resolution, not capability. A small tube in a YZ15 delivers less volume per revolution, so a single step of the motor moves a smaller quantity of fluid. That makes the YZ15 the better tool for precise, low-flow metering: reagent addition, media dispensing, analytical dosing. The same precision at higher flows simply requires more speed.
        </p>
        <p>
          The YZ35's larger bore moves more fluid per revolution, which makes it inherently coarser at the low end but extremely effective where repeatable high-volume delivery is the goal — batch filling, transfer, circulation. In both cases, accuracy depends far more on tube condition and drive quality than on the head itself: a worn or incorrectly sized tube erodes precision no matter which head you choose. If your process needs strict dosing accuracy in the low-flow range, pair the YZ15 with a stepper drive and keep the tubing on a replacement schedule.
        </p>

        <h2 id="applications">Application Fit: Laboratory Dosing vs Industrial Transfer</h2>
        <p>
          The two heads have become shorthand for two application worlds, and the shorthand is mostly accurate. The <strong>YZ15</strong> is the default for laboratory and light-duty work: pumping culture media, reagents, buffers and samples; filling tubes and vials; feeding analyzers and dispensers; OEM integration into benchtop instruments. Its small-tube range, fine flow control and compact, low-cost design map directly onto equipment where volume is modest and accuracy matters.
        </p>
        <p>
          The <strong>YZ35</strong> is the industrial-transfer head: moving water, chemicals, slurries and viscous fluids in larger batches, filling drums and tanks, circulating coolant or CIP solution, transferring fluids in food, water-treatment and light process settings. Because the fluid only ever touches the tube interior, both heads handle corrosive and abrasive media gracefully — the head materials and tube material decide compatibility, not the pump mechanism. If your duty is continuous, high-volume and less precision-sensitive, the YZ35's bigger bore means fewer tube revolutions per liter, which typically translates to longer tube life per unit of fluid moved.
        </p>

        <h2 id="cost">Cost and Price Positioning</h2>
        <p>
          On purchase price, the YZ15 is usually the cheaper head: it is smaller, uses less material, and its tubing is inexpensive per meter. For a laboratory or OEM budget, the YZ15 is the low-entry-cost route into peristaltic pumping, and replacement tubes for 13#–19# sizes cost little to stock. The YZ35 commands a higher upfront price, justified by larger machining, bigger rollers and the heavier-duty construction needed to squeeze large-bore tubing.
        </p>
        <p>
          Total cost of ownership changes the picture slightly. At high throughput, the YZ35's larger tube moves far more fluid per meter of tubing, so consumable cost per liter can actually favor the bigger head — you replace tubing less often relative to volume moved. In low-volume laboratory work, that economy never materializes, and the YZ15 with cheap small-bore tubing is the sensible choice. When comparing quotes, ask suppliers for the head price together with a tube price list for your size — the pair is what matters, not the head alone.
        </p>

