        <h2 id="choose">How to Choose: 5-Point Decision Checklist</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>If</strong> your required flow is under roughly 500 ml/min — <strong>then</strong> choose the YZ15; it gives you a wide usable speed window and fine control where precision dosing lives.</li>
          <li><strong>If</strong> you need liters-per-minute transfer (1 L/min or more sustained) — <strong>then</strong> choose the YZ35; its large-bore tubing delivers high volume without pushing the drive to its limit.</li>
          <li><strong>If</strong> your tubing size is 19# or smaller — <strong>then</strong> choose the YZ15; small tubes sit poorly in a large-bore track and produce unstable occlusion and flow.</li>
          <li><strong>If</strong> you plan to run 25# or larger tubing — <strong>then</strong> confirm the head: YZ15 covers 25# in most designs, but 35#-class tubing requires a YZ35.</li>
          <li><strong>If</strong> precision low-flow dosing is the priority and budget is tight — <strong>then</strong> choose the YZ15 with a stepper drive and fresh small-bore tubing, and keep spare tube stock small and cheap.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
        <h3>Can a YZ35 head also run 13#–19# small tubing?</h3>
        <p>Generally not well. The YZ35's track, roller spacing and occlusion are machined for large-bore tubing, and small tubes in a big track typically produce inconsistent occlusion, unstable flow and premature tube wear. If your application is small-tube dosing, the YZ15 is the right head.</p>
        <h3>Which head is more accurate, YZ15 or YZ35?</h3>
        <p>Both deliver around ±1% repeatability with a stepper drive and fresh tubing. The YZ15 offers finer resolution at low flow because a small tube moves less volume per revolution; the YZ35 is better suited to repeatable high-volume delivery. Accuracy depends mostly on tube condition, sizing and drive quality.</p>
        <h3>How much flow can each head actually deliver?</h3>
        <p>On a typical 0.1–600 rpm drive, a YZ15 spans from well under 1 ml/min up to roughly 4,500 ml/min depending on tube size and speed. A YZ35 with 35#-class tubing is commonly rated to around 12,000 ml/min. Exact numbers come from the manufacturer's flow matrix for your tube size and drive speed.</p>
        <h3>Do YZ15 and YZ35 mount on the same pump drive?</h3>
        <p>In most designs, yes — both use the same standard mounting interface and shaft types, so one drive can run either head. Just confirm the drive's torque and speed range: a YZ35 squeezing a large tube needs more torque than a YZ15 with 13# tubing, especially at low speed.</p>
        <h3>Is the YZ35 always better for industrial use?</h3>
        <p>Not automatically. Industrial use means higher volume, which favors the YZ35, but many industrial processes are precision dosing at modest flows — and there the YZ15 (or an LP-style head) is the better fit. Choose by flow and tube size first, and by industry label second.</p>

        <h2 id="conclusion">Conclusion</h2>
        <p>
          The YZ15 and YZ35 are two ends of the same dependable YZ family, and the decision between them is mostly a flow-and-tube question. If your process lives in the milliliters range, uses 13#–19# tubing, and rewards fine control — laboratory work, dosing, filling, OEM equipment — the YZ15 is the sensible, lower-cost choice. If you are moving liters per minute with large-bore tubing, the YZ35 is the head that keeps drive speed reasonable and consumable cost per liter low.
        </p>
